Mitcad's patch to OCCT 8.0.1, under OCCT's licence:
SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-2.1-only WITH OCCT-exception-1.0

Fillets: a closed rounding whose start overflowed a face of its edges all
round (the face lies wholly between the blend's contacts and vanishes),
and whose contact on the face beyond crosses a hole with a rounded rim:
0160's walk over the rim for such stripes, and the ends of the walked
pieces where the other contact crosses a seam (mitcad#261). E.g. a disc
whose bottom edge was rounded with 1 and which was then turned down by 0.1,
the edge between the turned cylinder and that rounding rounded with 1: the
ball touches the cylinder and the bottom, the earlier rounding lies wholly
between the contacts, and the contact on the bottom crosses a hole whose
rim is rounded with 0.5.

- ChFi3d_ConsumedSpineFaces (new): the faces of the edges of a closed
  spine on which no surface data of the stripe lies; the contacts left
  them all round, so the blend consumes them. The topological build drops
  them once their neighbours are cut (no face kept holds their edges).
- ChFi3d_Builder::WalkedBridgeOverGap: the walk over the gap is also taken
  where the face the contact runs on is not a face of the spine's edges but,
  on a closed spine, a face beyond such a consumed face. Before, it was
  refused (the result kept the consumed face) and the fillet failed with
  "Chaining is impossible". The edges of the rim's face with a consumed
  face vanish with it: a vertex of the rim under the blend at such an edge
  needs no edge grown (ChFi3d_KPartCutPiece's CutEdges).
- WalkedBridgeOverGap: a stretch ends where the other contact reaches an
  edge of its face (a seam); its cut piece ended at the section through the
  spine's point there (as for analytic blends), not at the walked piece's
  own end section (the rolling ball's sections are not in the guide's
  normal planes: 0.011 off on the case), and its point on the seam was
  looked up on the seam's first occurrence in the face. The cylinder's
  seam then kept its piece under the blend, and with it the part of the
  cylinder and the consumed face. ChFi3d_KPartCutPiece (AtFullEnds) ends a
  piece at an end of the walked piece at that piece's own section
  (ChFi3d_KPartSectionCutAt) with its common point on the arc, as the walk
  found it; the next stretch starts on the arc (RecOnS, as a walk goes on
  across a seam).
